{"id":5916,"date":"2022-03-02T14:02:22","date_gmt":"2022-03-02T22:02:22","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=5916"},"modified":"2025-10-23T13:41:18","modified_gmt":"2025-10-23T20:41:18","slug":"3-6-front-range-sensor-1-cde-blockly","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-6-front-range-sensor-1-cde-blockly\/","title":{"rendered":"3.6: Front Range Sensor"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcda40\" name=\"6a22137fcda40\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>We've Moved!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1761252076376{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-11766\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"81\" height=\"82\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: <a href=\"http:\/\/learn.robolink.com\">Robolink Learn<\/a>! Lessons here will remain available until the end of June 2026. This version of Basecamp will no longer be updated and uses outdated references to Blockly for Robolink. You can access the previous version of <a href=\"https:\/\/codrone.robolink.com\/edu\/blockly-archive\/\">Blockly here<\/a>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcdbc3\" name=\"6a22137fcdbc3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How Does the Front Range Sensor Work?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The front range sensor on your drone is the same sensor as the one on the bottom. Just like it was mentioned in the previous lesson, you will see two dots on the sensor. One of those emits the infrared light, and the other receives the infrared that is reflected from the surface. The chip calculates the distance based on the time it took for the infrared light to come back to the receiver.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<strong>Note: The maximum range for the front range sensor is 1.5 meters.<\/strong>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;9443&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcdc53\" name=\"6a22137fcdc53\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Introduction to the Front Range Sensor<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"8\">You can access the front range sensor data with the\u00a0<strong><code>get_range()<\/code><\/strong> block in the &#8220;Sensors&#8221; menu. Start by printing the front range sensor value. Go to the &#8220;Sensors&#8221; menu and drag the <strong><code>get_range()<\/code><\/strong>\u00a0function into your workspace.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8901&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"13\">To see the values, attach a print block. Then, add a\u00a0<strong><code>sleep(0.5)<\/code><\/strong>\u00a0<strong>sec<\/strong> block from the &#8220;Timing&#8221; category. Run all of this in a loop to repeat 20 times.<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"16\">While running the program, slowly move your hand toward and away from the front range sensor.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10621&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Try changing the units by selecting millimeters or inches from the dropdown menu and repeating the experiment. As a bonus, grab a ruler or meter stick to measure the accuracy of the sensor.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10622&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcdcd1\" name=\"6a22137fcdcd1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Safe Takeoff<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Pilots often do a series of pre-flight checks to make sure that everything is working on their aircraft before attempting to fly. One thing to consider checking is making sure that the takeoff area is clear. Since CoDrone EDU has a front range sensor, you can automate this process! For our next program, the drone will only take off if there is not an object within 30cm (about 12 inches) in front of the drone.<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div>Start by making a variable called <strong><code>distance<\/code><\/strong>. This variable will store the minimum distance in centimeters that should be clear in front of the drone. Since you only want the drone to take off if the there is no object in front within 30 centimeters, set the variable to 30.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10623&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Before taking off, use an if statement to check if the area is clear by comparing the data from the front range sensor (in centimeters) to the variable. Write a warning message if the sensor is detecting an object less than 30 centimeters away.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12941&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The if statement below will only return true if there is an object within 30 centimeters from the front of the drone. If that is not true, program an else statement to take off and then land. Make another print statement to state that the drone has been cleared for takeoff.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12943&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcdd56\" name=\"6a22137fcdd56\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Avoid an Obstacle During Flight<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"42\">Now that preflight checks are complete, add in some flight movements! If the area is clear for takeoff. CoDrone EDU will take off and fly forward until it detects another obstacle.<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"44\">You are going to add code to the else statement. Since you don\u2019t know how long it will take for our drone to find an obstacle, include an infinite while loop. Add a while loop after takeoff.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12944&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]While the sensor is reading any value greater than 30 centimeters, CoDrone EDU should keep flying. You can copy and paste the same conditional from earlier, <em>but don\u2019t forget to change the less\/greater than sign the opposite way!<\/em>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12945&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Add in the flight movement for CoDrone EDU to fly forward while there is no obstacle ahead. Since we are relying on sensor information to detect an obstacle, fly forward with precaution at a lower speed. Then, add a <strong><code>move()<\/code><\/strong>block without the seconds delay. If you add a delay, it will slow down the program, and you might crash into an obstacle before the sensor was able to detect it in time.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12946&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Finally, outside the loop, add a print statement and land. Here is the final code:<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12947&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcddd2\" name=\"6a22137fcddd2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenges<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Drone Basketball<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>For this challenge, make your own basketball hoop using a wall and hula-hoop or other materials. Your goal is to have CoDrone EDU go through the hoop after detecting the &#8216;backboard&#8217;, just like a basketball would! In order to complete the challenge, you must do the following:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create your own basketball hoop for CoDrone EDU to fly through<\/li>\n<li>Your program must have the CoDrone EDU fly towards the hoop, detect the wall\/backboard, and fly through the hoop before landing<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7761&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Homing in<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this challenge, you will be programming your CoDrone EDU to fly as close to the wall as possible! Using the front range sensor, try and write a program that will stop the CoDrone once it reaches a certain distance from the wall; that distance will depend on where you are flying your CoDrone EDU, so make sure to test out different values!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137fcde9e\" name=\"6a22137fcde9e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>In this lesson, you learned how to use the CoDrone EDU\u2019s front range sensor to detect how far an object is in front of the CoDrone EDU. It has many useful applications like building autonomous navigation programs and safety features.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7547&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 We've Moved! [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1761252076376{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: Robolink Learn! Lessons here&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-6-front-range-sensor-1-cde-blockly\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4742,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3877,"_lesson_preview":""},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2195,2108,4328,14401,2189],"class_list":["post-5916","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-sensors","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/5916","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}]